OVERARCHING IDEA:
A device thate d generally helps an individual to collect data points on their own qualitative lived experience of being in their body. 
A cyber-physical device that allows for the collection of personalised data from a: sensors and b: self-reported perception of experience via a hardware interface of buttons.

CPS design notes:
A watch like wrist/arm worn interface. 
With hardware buttons that each cover a specific area of complaint/symptom. 
These buttons also lights up. 

This allows for a feedback looping interface that functions as such:

1. Watch is loaded with sensor data allowing the device to compare averaged sensor baselines with the sensor feedback behaviour worn by the individual.
2. When the sensors read spikes that correlate with the symptomatic avaraged feedback that is attributed with those symptoms the user wishes to track, the light for the corresponding symptom of the watch face lights up as a point of actuation.
3. This serves multiple purposes 
      1. getting the attention of the user. 
      2. increasing potential for adherence to the desired behaviour AKA registering self-perceived symptomatic events in the body.
      3. The user will see the button light up, click the button if they agree, and then choose a button down the centre of the watch face rated from 1-5 on scale of the strength of that symptom. 

4. This then allows the user to gather self-referrential longitudinal data that can be provided to a medical professional of other allied health care provider.
5. The systems records these points of information
      1. The users sensor feedback
      2. The contrast between the user feedback and the average feedback from these sensors.
      3. The users self-reported input of validation of spikes in sensor behaviour in response to the sensor actuation.
      4. The users self-reported input of symptomatic spikes without being a response to actuation by the system seeking a response to a spike. 
6. This data can then be used to produce a data set that can be used in the following ways:
      1. provided to a healthcare professional for increased agency in the self-reporting process when trying to gain support from a doctor.
      2. to help the user see for themselves if there are trends in their self-identified symptoms, to increase self-validation of lived experience.
      3. assist in supporting the doctor/patient with bridging the divide is experienced/perceived pain from the perspective of the patient/doctor.
      4. Personalised training of an AI model. which could help the user to see their own biofeedback based on their personalised data alone. 
      5. Used to trian a model to adjust the baselines averages of actuation thresholds in the watch over time (like the weighted actuation changes in the Pear Pie machine learning) Maybe can reuse the code??
